<body>
<h1>Planning regression studies with regsimsize</h1>
<p class="meta">Yasser C Bouklouch | Version 0.1.1 | MIT License</p>
<p>Build a virtual study, analyze it as planned, and count how often the chosen
hypothesis is detected. Repeat this at several sample sizes to see how much
information your study needs.</p>
<p class="note">The examples below use eight simulations per sample size to run
quickly. Their numerical results demonstrate the software. They are too imprecise
to choose a real study's sample size.</p>

<!--begin.rcode setup, include=FALSE
library(regsimsize)
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", error = FALSE)
end.rcode-->

<h2>1. Specify the study and the question</h2>
<p>A design combines three things: a data generator, a model fitter, and a test.
The built-in constructors supply the first two for common models. You choose
the hypothesis. Sample size means the number passed to the generator, so define
whether it counts people, clusters, or grouped observations.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Analysis</th><th>Constructor</th><th>What you supply</th></tr>
<tr><td>Logistic and other GLMs</td><td><code>glm_design()</code></td><td>Formula, coefficients, covariates, family, and test</td></tr>
<tr><td>Linear regression</td><td><code>lm_design()</code></td><td>The same, plus residual variability</td></tr>
<tr><td>Right-censored Cox regression</td><td><code>cox_design()</code></td><td>Hazard ratios, baseline hazard, censoring, follow-up, and test</td></tr>
<tr><td>Other models or study structures</td><td><code>simulation_design()</code></td><td>Data generator, fitting function, and one valid p-value</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The package estimates power for a specified hypothesis. A significant
coefficient alone does not establish that a prediction model has enough data
for stable development. Prediction planning needs criteria for overfitting,
calibration, and precision of predictions, as provided by methods such as
<a href="https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=pmsampsize">pmsampsize</a>.</p>

<h2>2. Logistic regression</h2>
<p>Here, treatment is balanced on average, age is standardized, and the target
treatment odds ratio is 1.8. Coefficients are entered on the log-odds scale.</p>
<!--begin.rcode logistic
covariates <- function(n) {
  data.frame(treatment = rbinom(n, 1, 0.5), age_z = rnorm(n))
}
logistic <- glm_design(
  outcome ~ treatment + age_z,
  coefficients = c("(Intercept)" = -1,
                   treatment = log(1.8), age_z = log(1.3)),
  covariates = covariates,
  test = wald_test("treatment"),
  family = binomial()
)
head(simulate_data(logistic, n = 150, seed = 8))
logistic_power <- power_curve(logistic, c(150, 300), nsim = 8, seed = 2026)
summary(logistic_power)
end.rcode-->
<p>Use <code>plot(logistic_power)</code> for the power curve and
<code>plot_event_rate(logistic_power)</code> for the simulated outcome fractions.
Results include errors and warnings at replicate level.</p>
<p>The intercept sets risk for the reference participant, not the population
average. To target an overall event probability of 20%, calibrate over a
representative covariate sample:</p>
<!--begin.rcode calibration
set.seed(16)
reference <- covariates(3000)
beta <- c("(Intercept)" = 0, treatment = log(1.8), age_z = log(1.3))
beta["(Intercept)"] <- calibrate_intercept(
  outcome ~ treatment + age_z, beta, reference, target = 0.20
)
beta
end.rcode-->
<p>Pass this adjusted vector to a new <code>glm_design()</code>. Calibration is
to the empirical reference distribution. Finite studies will vary around that
event proportion.</p>

<h3>Factors, interactions, and grouped binomial outcomes</h3>
<p>Coefficient names follow R's model matrix. Set factor levels explicitly and
use <code>model_coefficients()</code> to avoid guessing names.</p>
<!--begin.rcode factors
factor_covariates <- function(n) {
  data.frame(age_z = rnorm(n),
    group = factor(sample(c("A", "B", "C"), n, replace = TRUE),
                   levels = c("A", "B", "C")))
}
set.seed(17)
f <- outcome ~ age_z * group
beta_factor <- model_coefficients(f, factor_covariates(100))
beta_factor["(Intercept)"] <- -1
beta_factor[c("age_z", "groupB", "groupC")] <- c(0.2, log(1.4), log(1.7))
beta_factor[c("age_z:groupB", "age_z:groupC")] <- c(0.1, -0.1)
interaction_design <- glm_design(f, beta_factor, factor_covariates,
  test = wald_test(c("age_z:groupB", "age_z:groupC")))

grouped <- glm_design(outcome ~ treatment + age_z,
  c("(Intercept)" = -1, treatment = log(1.5), age_z = 0.2),
  covariates, test = wald_test("treatment"), trials = 5)
head(simulate_data(grouped, 100, seed = 9)$outcome)
end.rcode-->
<p>The grouped response has successes and failures in two columns. Here
<code>n = 100</code> means 100 covariate rows with 500 total trials. The overall
event fraction uses the total trials as its denominator. A test of both
interaction coefficients answers a different question from testing either
coefficient individually.</p>
<p>Probit, complementary log-log, and other compatible links can be passed
through <code>binomial(link = ...)</code>. Poisson, Gaussian, Gamma, and
inverse-Gaussian families have built-in response generators. Quasi-families
require an explicit generator because a mean-variance relationship does not
specify a sampling distribution.</p>

<h2>3. Linear and weighted linear regression</h2>
<p>Specify the mean outcome through the formula and coefficients. The default
residual distribution is normal with standard deviation <code>sigma</code>.</p>
<!--begin.rcode linear
linear <- lm_design(outcome ~ treatment + age_z,
  c("(Intercept)" = 0, treatment = 0.5, age_z = 0.2),
  covariates, test = wald_test("treatment"), sigma = 1)
linear_power <- power_curve(linear, c(80, 160), nsim = 8, seed = 21)
print(linear_power)

weighted <- lm_design(outcome ~ treatment + age_z,
  c("(Intercept)" = 0, treatment = 0.5, age_z = 0.2),
  covariates, test = wald_test("treatment"),
  sigma = function(data) sqrt(1 + data$age_z^2),
  fit_args = function(data) list(weights = 1 / (1 + data$age_z^2)))
end.rcode-->
<p>In the weighted example, the generating variance and analysis weights agree.
They can be varied independently to investigate misspecification. A custom
<code>error(n, data)</code> callback allows other residual distributions.
For ordinary linear fits, the built-in coefficient test uses the residual
degrees of freedom and a t reference distribution.</p>

<h2>4. Cox regression</h2>
<p>Cox planning needs both recruitment and event assumptions. Coefficients
are log hazard ratios. Follow-up and baseline hazard must use the same time unit.</p>
<!--begin.rcode cox
cox <- cox_design(
  survival::Surv(time, status) ~ treatment + age_z,
  coefficients = c(treatment = log(0.65), age_z = log(1.2)),
  covariates = covariates,
  test = wald_test("treatment"),
  baseline = weibull_baseline(rate = 0.12, shape = 1.2),
  censoring = function(n, data) rexp(n, rate = 0.03),
  followup = 5
)
cox_power <- power_curve(cox, c(150, 300), nsim = 8, seed = 22)
print(cox_power)
end.rcode-->
<p>For this Weibull parameterization, the cumulative baseline hazard is
<code>rate * time^shape</code>. The generator inverts the cumulative hazard,
then applies dropout and administrative censoring. Alternatives include
<code>piecewise_baseline()</code> and a user-defined inverse cumulative hazard.</p>
<h3>Stratified analysis needs stratified generation</h3>
<!--begin.rcode stratified
stratum_covariates <- function(n) {
  d <- covariates(n)
  d$site <- factor(sample(c("A", "B"), n, replace = TRUE),
                   levels = c("A", "B"))
  d
}
stratified <- cox_design(
  survival::Surv(time, status) ~ treatment + age_z + strata(site),
  c(treatment = log(0.7), age_z = 0.2), stratum_covariates,
  test = wald_test("treatment"), lp_formula = ~ treatment + age_z,
  baseline = weibull_baseline(rate = function(d) ifelse(d$site == "A", 0.1, 0.2)),
  followup = 5)
end.rcode-->
<p>The analysis formula retains the strata; the generating linear predictor
excludes them because the baseline callback supplies the stratum-specific
hazards. Frailty, delayed entry, recurrent events, time-varying effects, and
competing-risk processes require explicit generators and suitable inference.
Use the general callback interface for those designs.</p>

<h2>5. Connect another model</h2>
<p>A general design takes functions with signatures
<code>generate(n)</code>, <code>fit(data)</code>, and <code>test(fit, data)</code>.
The test must return one p-value, or a list containing <code>p.value</code>.</p>
<!--begin.rcode callback
custom <- simulation_design(
  generate = function(n) {
    d <- data.frame(x = rnorm(n))
    d$y <- 0.4 * d$x + rnorm(n)
    d
  },
  fit = function(data) lm(y ~ x, data = data),
  test = function(fit, data) coef(summary(fit))["x", "Pr(>|t|)"],
  label = "Custom linear model"
)
system.file("examples", "advanced-designs.R", package = "regsimsize")
end.rcode-->
<p>The installed advanced examples define time-varying Cox, recurrent-event
Cox, shared gamma-frailty Cox, and random-intercept linear designs. They show
how to connect each analysis and define its sampling unit. They do not validate
every possible use of those models. Penalized fits, boundary tests, and
post-selection inference require methods appropriate to their targets.</p>
<p>Use a joint Wald test for a multi-coefficient hypothesis or
<code>lrt_test(reduced_formula)</code> for a valid nested likelihood comparison.
The built-in LRT rejects quasi-likelihood and cluster-robust Cox comparisons.
An ordinary LRT is asymptotic, including for Gaussian linear models; a Wald/F
test provides the finite-sample alternative under the linear model assumptions.</p>

<h2>6. Interpret power and select a sample size</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Result</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>power</code></td><td>Valid rejections divided by all attempted analyses</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>failed</code>, <code>failure_rate</code></td><td>Analyses that could not produce an accepted valid test</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>power_successful</code></td><td>Power conditional on successful analysis</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>lower</code>, <code>upper</code></td><td>Pointwise Wilson limits for Monte Carlo uncertainty</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>mcse</code></td><td>Estimated Monte Carlo standard error</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>mean_events</code></td><td>Mean generated event count, including datasets whose analyses failed</td></tr>
</table>
<p>By default, warnings make an analysis fail. Inspect
<code>logistic_power$replicates</code> to see messages and stages. The explicit
option <code>warning_policy = "record"</code> accepts warning-producing
analyses only when the test is valid and built-in convergence checks pass.
Review the warnings before choosing this option.</p>
<p><code>select_sample_size()</code> defaults to the smallest evaluated sample
size whose lower Monte Carlo confidence limit reaches the target and whose
observed failure rate is at most 1%. It can return no recommendation. It does
not extrapolate beyond the supplied grid.</p>
<pre><code># Run only when ready for a planning calculation:
curve &lt;- power_curve(logistic, seq(200, 1600, 200),
                     nsim = 5000, seed = 100)
candidate &lt;- select_sample_size(curve, target = 0.80)
if (!is.na(candidate$n)) {
  confirmation &lt;- power_curve(logistic, candidate$n,
                              nsim = 10000, seed = 101)
  print(confirmation)
}</code></pre>
<p>These larger runs are shown for real planning and are not executed by this
guide. Confirm a selected candidate independently. Pointwise intervals do not
provide a simultaneous guarantee after searching many sample sizes.</p>
<!--begin.rcode precision
mc_replicates(half_width = 0.01, power = 0.8)
end.rcode-->
<p>More simulations reduce simulation error. They do not resolve uncertainty
about an assumed treatment effect, recruitment, censoring, or measurement bias.
Repeat planning across plausible scenarios and check type I error under a null
scenario using the intended model and test.</p>

<h2>7. Reproduce, export, and report</h2>
<p>Each replicate receives its own L'Ecuyer-CMRG random-number stream. The caller's
random-number state is restored. Given the same software, callbacks, seed,
sample size grid, and replicate count, sequential and parallel runs produce
the same raw results. Changing the grid or replicate count changes later
stream assignments. Worker callbacks must have their dependencies available
and must not reset their own seed.</p>
<pre><code>parallel_curve &lt;- power_curve(logistic, c(150, 300),
                              nsim = 8, seed = 2026, workers = 2)
saveRDS(parallel_curve, "planning-results.rds")
write.csv(as.data.frame(parallel_curve), "power-summary.csv", row.names = FALSE)</code></pre>
<p>Report the generating assumptions, sampling unit, model and test, significance
level, simulation count, seed, failure handling, Monte Carlo uncertainty, and
selection rule. The installed <code>doc/methods.md</code> explains these choices;
<code>examples/statistical-validation.R</code> contains optional statistical
benchmarks. See the repository validation record for the checks performed and their limits.</p>
<h3>References and software citation</h3>
<p>Morris TP, White IR, Crowther MJ (2019). Using simulation studies to evaluate
statistical methods. <em>Statistics in Medicine</em> 38:2074-2102.
<a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/sim.8086">doi:10.1002/sim.8086</a>.</p>
